# 6. Django ALLOWED_HOSTS

Update Django configuration so the API accepts:

1. localhost
2. 127.0.0.1
3. local LAN access
4. the configured public API hostname

Use environment variables.

Example:

```env
DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost,127.0.0.1,api.example.com
```

Do NOT use:

```python
ALLOWED_HOSTS = ["*"]
```

for production configuration.

---

# 7. CSRF Configuration

If Django uses session authentication or Django admin, configure trusted origins correctly.

Use an environment variable such as:

```env
DJANGO_CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://api.example.com
```

Do not blindly allow every origin.

---

# 8. CORS

If the project already uses `django-cors-headers`, update it correctly.

Support:

```env
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=https://your-vercel-app.vercel.app
```

and allow the local frontend when necessary.

Do NOT use:

```python
CORS_ALLOW_ALL_ORIGINS = True
```

in production.

Make the configuration environment-based.

Example:

```env
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000,https://your-vercel-app.vercel.app
```

The actual Vercel URL must be configurable.

